1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,359
Last lesson you built a clean Python project.

2
00:00:03,359 --> 00:00:06,217
Today you write the code that goes inside it.

3
00:00:06,767 --> 00:00:12,527
Five building blocks. Types. Collections. Conditions and loops. Functions. And modules.

4
00:00:13,077 --> 00:00:17,749
Every demo is typed live on Ubuntu twenty-four oh four, so you can follow along.

5
00:00:19,799 --> 00:00:23,916
Why Python for DevOps? Shell is great for gluing commands together.

6
00:00:24,466 --> 00:00:26,883
But the moment your data has structure,

7
00:00:26,883 --> 00:00:27,999
a list of servers,

8
00:00:27,999 --> 00:00:29,115
a map of settings,

9
00:00:29,115 --> 00:00:30,478
an answer from an API,

10
00:00:30,478 --> 00:00:31,656
shell gets painful.

11
00:00:32,206 --> 00:00:36,643
Python gives you clear data types, readable errors, and code you can test.

12
00:00:37,193 --> 00:00:42,932
And a lot of DevOps data is already in the shape Python understands: lists and dictionaries.

13
00:00:43,482 --> 00:00:47,770
So these five building blocks are the base for every tool in this masterclass.

14
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:50,266
First,

15
00:00:50,266 --> 00:00:50,712
types.

16
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:53,090
Start Python's interactive mode,

17
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:54,501
called the R E P L.

18
00:00:54,501 --> 00:00:55,913
Type: python three,

19
00:00:55,913 --> 00:00:56,433
dash Q.

20
00:00:57,983 --> 00:01:01,103
Three greater-than signs is the Python prompt.

21
00:01:01,103 --> 00:01:02,460
Store a port number.

22
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:03,206
Type: port,

23
00:01:03,206 --> 00:01:03,681
equals,

24
00:01:03,681 --> 00:01:05,173
eight zero eight zero.

25
00:01:05,723 --> 00:01:06,895
Nothing printed,

26
00:01:06,895 --> 00:01:08,360
the value is stored.

27
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,044
Every value has a type.

28
00:01:10,044 --> 00:01:10,850
Ask Python.

29
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:11,656
Type: type,

30
00:01:11,656 --> 00:01:12,608
open bracket,

31
00:01:12,608 --> 00:01:12,974
port,

32
00:01:12,974 --> 00:01:14,000
close bracket.

33
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:19,947
Class int, a whole number. Now some text. Type: name, equals, quote, web one, quote.

34
00:01:20,497 --> 00:01:26,172
Text goes in quotes. Ask its type. Type: type, open bracket, name, close bracket.

35
00:01:26,722 --> 00:01:27,435
Class str,

36
00:01:27,435 --> 00:01:28,076
a string.

37
00:01:28,076 --> 00:01:34,777
Now a trap that bites DevOps engineers all the time: environment variables are always strings.

38
00:01:34,777 --> 00:01:37,130
Compare the number with the text.

39
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:37,914
Type: port,

40
00:01:37,914 --> 00:01:38,912
equals equals,

41
00:01:38,912 --> 00:01:39,339
quote,

42
00:01:39,339 --> 00:01:40,908
eight zero eight zero,

43
00:01:40,908 --> 00:01:41,335
quote.

44
00:01:41,885 --> 00:01:42,297
False.

45
00:01:42,297 --> 00:01:47,780
The number eight thousand eighty is not equal to the text eight thousand eighty.

46
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:48,946
Convert the text.

47
00:01:48,946 --> 00:01:49,631
Type: int,

48
00:01:49,631 --> 00:01:50,522
open bracket,

49
00:01:50,522 --> 00:01:50,933
quote,

50
00:01:50,933 --> 00:01:52,441
eight zero eight zero,

51
00:01:52,441 --> 00:01:52,853
quote,

52
00:01:52,853 --> 00:01:53,812
close bracket,

53
00:01:53,812 --> 00:01:54,429
plus one.

54
00:01:54,979 --> 00:01:56,913
Eight thousand eighty-one.

55
00:01:56,913 --> 00:01:59,070
Int turns text into a number.

56
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:00,409
Leave the R E P L.

57
00:02:00,409 --> 00:02:01,227
Type: exit,

58
00:02:01,227 --> 00:02:02,194
open bracket,

59
00:02:02,194 --> 00:02:03,235
close bracket.

60
00:02:04,385 --> 00:02:05,931
Remember: types matter.

61
00:02:05,931 --> 00:02:08,284
Use int to turn text into a number,

62
00:02:08,284 --> 00:02:10,636
and str to turn a number into text.

63
00:02:12,686 --> 00:02:17,123
Next, collections. Start Python again. Type: python three, dash Q.

64
00:02:18,673 --> 00:02:20,564
A list holds items in order.

65
00:02:20,564 --> 00:02:21,374
Type: ports,

66
00:02:21,374 --> 00:02:21,847
equals,

67
00:02:21,847 --> 00:02:22,860
square bracket,

68
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:23,333
eighty,

69
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:23,738
comma,

70
00:02:23,738 --> 00:02:24,886
four forty-three,

71
00:02:24,886 --> 00:02:25,291
comma,

72
00:02:25,291 --> 00:02:26,237
eighty eighty,

73
00:02:26,237 --> 00:02:27,655
close square bracket.

74
00:02:28,205 --> 00:02:33,410
Add an item. Type: ports dot append, open bracket, twenty-two, close bracket.

75
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:35,901
Look at the list. Type: ports.

76
00:02:36,451 --> 00:02:37,279
Four items.

77
00:02:37,279 --> 00:02:39,084
Counting starts at zero,

78
00:02:39,084 --> 00:02:41,490
so the first item is index zero.

79
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:42,393
Type: ports,

80
00:02:42,393 --> 00:02:43,521
square bracket,

81
00:02:43,521 --> 00:02:43,897
zero.

82
00:02:44,447 --> 00:02:44,964
Eighty.

83
00:02:44,964 --> 00:02:48,143
Now a dictionary: names paired with values,

84
00:02:48,143 --> 00:02:49,326
in curly braces.

85
00:02:49,326 --> 00:02:50,287
Type: server,

86
00:02:50,287 --> 00:02:50,805
equals,

87
00:02:50,805 --> 00:02:51,249
brace,

88
00:02:51,249 --> 00:02:52,505
quote name quote,

89
00:02:52,505 --> 00:02:52,949
colon,

90
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:54,428
quote web one quote,

91
00:02:54,428 --> 00:02:54,871
comma,

92
00:02:54,871 --> 00:02:56,202
quote C P U quote,

93
00:02:56,202 --> 00:02:56,646
colon,

94
00:02:56,646 --> 00:02:57,533
seventy-two,

95
00:02:57,533 --> 00:02:58,420
close brace.

96
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:00,938
Look up a value by its name.

97
00:03:00,938 --> 00:03:01,852
Type: server,

98
00:03:01,852 --> 00:03:02,906
square bracket,

99
00:03:02,906 --> 00:03:04,171
quote C P U quote,

100
00:03:04,171 --> 00:03:05,647
close square bracket.

101
00:03:06,197 --> 00:03:06,993
Seventy-two.

102
00:03:06,993 --> 00:03:08,652
What if a key is missing?

103
00:03:08,652 --> 00:03:10,708
Square brackets raise an error,

104
00:03:10,708 --> 00:03:12,831
so use get with a default value.

105
00:03:12,831 --> 00:03:14,224
Type: server dot get,

106
00:03:14,224 --> 00:03:15,086
open bracket,

107
00:03:15,086 --> 00:03:16,147
quote mem quote,

108
00:03:16,147 --> 00:03:16,545
comma,

109
00:03:16,545 --> 00:03:18,005
quote N slash A quote,

110
00:03:18,005 --> 00:03:18,933
close bracket.

111
00:03:19,483 --> 00:03:20,174
N slash A,

112
00:03:20,174 --> 00:03:21,003
the default.

113
00:03:21,003 --> 00:03:22,523
Now the error version.

114
00:03:22,523 --> 00:03:23,421
Type: server,

115
00:03:23,421 --> 00:03:24,458
square bracket,

116
00:03:24,458 --> 00:03:25,563
quote mem quote,

117
00:03:25,563 --> 00:03:27,014
close square bracket.

118
00:03:27,564 --> 00:03:28,352
A key error.

119
00:03:28,352 --> 00:03:30,782
Python names exactly what is missing.

120
00:03:30,782 --> 00:03:31,899
Lists keep order,

121
00:03:31,899 --> 00:03:34,526
and dictionaries look things up by name.

122
00:03:34,526 --> 00:03:36,430
You will use both constantly,

123
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:40,108
because the answer from an API is made of exactly these.

124
00:03:42,158 --> 00:03:43,459
Now a real script,

125
00:03:43,459 --> 00:03:45,482
with a condition and a loop.

126
00:03:45,482 --> 00:03:48,083
Here is a file called check dot P Y.

127
00:03:48,083 --> 00:03:49,817
Type: cat check dot P Y.

128
00:03:50,367 --> 00:03:53,703
A list of pairs: a server name and its C P U.

129
00:03:53,703 --> 00:03:55,779
A for loop visits each pair.

130
00:03:55,779 --> 00:03:56,298
Inside,

131
00:03:56,298 --> 00:03:58,449
if the C P U is above eighty,

132
00:03:58,449 --> 00:03:59,264
print busy,

133
00:03:59,264 --> 00:04:00,451
else print okay.

134
00:04:00,451 --> 00:04:02,453
Notice there are no braces.

135
00:04:02,453 --> 00:04:04,232
Python uses indentation,

136
00:04:04,232 --> 00:04:05,122
four spaces,

137
00:04:05,122 --> 00:04:07,198
to show what belongs inside.

138
00:04:07,198 --> 00:04:07,717
Run it.

139
00:04:07,717 --> 00:04:09,126
Type: python three,

140
00:04:09,126 --> 00:04:10,164
check dot P Y.

141
00:04:10,714 --> 00:04:11,728
Web two is busy,

142
00:04:11,728 --> 00:04:13,249
and the others are okay.

143
00:04:13,249 --> 00:04:14,833
The loop ran three times,

144
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:17,114
and the if chose a branch each time.

145
00:04:17,664 --> 00:04:19,082
Two rules to remember.

146
00:04:19,082 --> 00:04:20,758
A colon opens every block,

147
00:04:20,758 --> 00:04:23,659
and the indented lines under it belong to it.

148
00:04:25,709 --> 00:04:29,130
Functions package logic so you can reuse it.

149
00:04:29,130 --> 00:04:30,840
Here is funcs dot P Y.

150
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:32,706
Type: cat funcs dot P Y.

151
00:04:33,256 --> 00:04:34,825
Def starts a function.

152
00:04:34,825 --> 00:04:36,394
Busy takes two inputs,

153
00:04:36,394 --> 00:04:38,534
a list of servers and a limit.

154
00:04:38,534 --> 00:04:40,388
It builds a list of names,

155
00:04:40,388 --> 00:04:41,457
and returns it.

156
00:04:41,457 --> 00:04:45,380
The last block runs only when the file is run directly.

157
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:45,879
Run it.

158
00:04:45,879 --> 00:04:47,234
Type: python three,

159
00:04:47,234 --> 00:04:48,232
funcs dot P Y.

160
00:04:48,782 --> 00:04:53,881
Only web two. Change the limit, and the answer changes. One function, many uses.

161
00:04:54,431 --> 00:04:56,258
A function should do one job,

162
00:04:56,258 --> 00:04:58,149
take its inputs as parameters,

163
00:04:58,149 --> 00:05:00,354
and give back a result with return.

164
00:05:00,354 --> 00:05:02,055
That makes it easy to test,

165
00:05:02,055 --> 00:05:03,946
which we do in a later lesson.

166
00:05:05,996 --> 00:05:06,619
Finally,

167
00:05:06,619 --> 00:05:09,264
modules: files of ready-made code.

168
00:05:09,264 --> 00:05:11,054
Python ships with many,

169
00:05:11,054 --> 00:05:13,233
called the standard library.

170
00:05:13,233 --> 00:05:16,579
Use J S O N to turn a dictionary into text.

171
00:05:16,579 --> 00:05:18,057
Type: python three,

172
00:05:18,057 --> 00:05:18,602
dash C,

173
00:05:18,602 --> 00:05:19,925
then import json,

174
00:05:19,925 --> 00:05:20,703
semicolon,

175
00:05:20,703 --> 00:05:21,170
print,

176
00:05:21,170 --> 00:05:22,337
json dot dumps.

177
00:05:22,887 --> 00:05:23,913
J S O N text,

178
00:05:23,913 --> 00:05:25,412
with double quotes,

179
00:05:25,412 --> 00:05:27,305
ready to send to an API.

180
00:05:27,305 --> 00:05:29,751
Your own files are modules too.

181
00:05:29,751 --> 00:05:30,776
Import funcs.

182
00:05:30,776 --> 00:05:32,275
Type: python three,

183
00:05:32,275 --> 00:05:32,827
dash C,

184
00:05:32,827 --> 00:05:33,853
import funcs.

185
00:05:34,403 --> 00:05:35,479
Nothing printed.

186
00:05:35,479 --> 00:05:40,325
The if name equals main guard kept the demo code from running on import.

187
00:05:40,325 --> 00:05:41,940
That is why we wrote it.

188
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:44,699
Now import something that does not exist.

189
00:05:44,699 --> 00:05:45,978
Type: python three,

190
00:05:45,978 --> 00:05:46,449
dash C,

191
00:05:46,449 --> 00:05:47,459
import nothere.

192
00:05:48,009 --> 00:05:49,184
Module not found.

193
00:05:49,184 --> 00:05:51,190
Inside a virtual environment,

194
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:54,026
that usually means a missing pip install,

195
00:05:54,026 --> 00:05:56,584
or an environment that is not active.

196
00:05:56,584 --> 00:05:58,590
Last lesson's habit pays off.

197
